<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kayqubad I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1237">1237</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Kaykubad I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kayqubad I was sultan of the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> from 1220 to 1237, the period of its greatest power. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alanya">Alanya</a>, which he rebuilt as a winter capital, extended Seljuk rule in eastern Anatolia, absorbing the Mengujekid lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzincan">Erzincan</a>, and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>, palaces and fortresses across his realm. In 1230 he defeated the Khwarazmian ruler Jalal al-Din.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kayqubad-i.jpg" alt="Kayqubad I" width="320" height="310" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: American Numismatic Society · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ala%27_al-Din_Kay_Qubad_I,_Ankuriyya,_1219-1237.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alā ad-Dīn Kayqubād was born about 1192, the second son of the Seljuk sultan Kaykhusraw, who gave him the title malik and the governorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokat">Tokat</a> while he was young. When the sultan died after the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alasehir">Alaşehir</a> in 1211, Kayqubad and his elder brother Kaykaus struggled for the throne.</p><p>Kayqubad had allies in Leo I, king of Cilician Armenia, and in his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughith-al-din-tughrilshah">Tughrilshah</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a>, but most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> supported Kaykaus. He fled to the fortress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> and sought help from the Turkman tribes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kastamonu">Kastamonu</a>, but was soon caught and imprisoned by his brother in a fortress in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Kaykaus died unexpectedly in 1219 or 1220, and Kayqubad was released and became sultan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Kayqubad expanded the sultanate at the expense of the Mengujek Beylik and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a>, and established a Seljuk presence on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> by taking the port of Kalon Oros, later renamed Ala'iyya after him. He reduced Cilician Armenia to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state and, in 1221 or 1222, launched a naval attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudak">Sudak</a> that defeated the combined forces of the Rus and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>. Jalal al-Din Mangburni, fleeing the Mongol destruction of the Khwarezmian Empire, unsettled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and Kayqubad first sought an alliance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>.</p><p>When it failed he defeated Jalal al-Din at the Battle of Yassıçimen, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzincan">Erzincan</a>, in 1230, and established Seljuk rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahlat">Ahlat</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-van">Lake Van</a> region. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artuqid-dynasty">Artuqids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> recognised his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. In 1234 he defeated the allied Ayyubid forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-kamil">al-Kamil</a>, took Siverek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanliurfa">Urfa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqa">Raqqa</a>, and forced the Komnenos dynasty of the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> to renew its pledges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a>. He also captured several fortresses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, and the queen of Georgia gave her daughter Tamar in marriage to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykhusraw-ii">Kaykhusraw II</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kayqubad died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayseri">Kayseri</a> on 31 May 1237, given poison at a feast; poison was later found in DNA analysis of his bones. He was the last of his line to die independent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykhusraw-ii">Kaykhusraw II</a> took the throne and had his half-brothers Rukn al-Din and Kilij Arslan and their mother strangled.</p><p>Kayqubad built a large number of mosques, medreses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>, bridges and hospitals, completed the Seljuk Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubadabad-palace">Kubadabad Palace</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-beysehir">Lake Beyşehir</a>, the castle and Red Tower at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alanya">Alanya</a> and the citadel walls of Konya. After the Mongol invasion the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> looked back on his reign as a golden age, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> rulers traced their pedigrees to him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kayqubad I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…but his sons expanded to the coasts: Kaykaus I took Sinop and captured the emperor of Trebizond, and <b>Kayqubad I</b> fortified Alanya, building its Red Tower and a shipyard, sent an expedition across the Black Sea to Sudak in the Crimea, and in 1230 defeated the Khwarazmian ruler…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Alanya was built up by the Seljuk sultan <b>Kayqubad I</b> after 1221 as a winter capital and naval base.</blockquote><a href="/state/alaiye-beylik">Alaiye Beylik</a></li><li><blockquote>Squeezed between the Ayyubids and the Sultanate of Rum, the Harput branch was annexed by the Seljuk sultan <b>Kayqubad I</b> in 1234.</blockquote><a href="/state/artuqids-of-harput">Artuqids of Harput</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sultanate of Rum absorbed the Erzincan branch around 1228 under <b>Kayqubad I</b>. The Divrigi branch survived as Seljuk and then Mongol vassals until it disappears from the record in 1277.</blockquote><a href="/state/mengujekids">Mengujekids</a></li></ul></section>
